Purpose: An index is a specialized data structure maintained alongside a database table to significantly accelerate data retrieval operations.
The Problem: Without an index, the database must perform a full table scan, which requires checking every single row, resulting in linear time complexity O(N).
The Solution: An index acts like the index at the back of a textbook, allowing the database to jump directly to the relevant data, reducing lookup time to logarithmic complexity O(log N).
Types of Indexes

B-Tree: The standard and most versatile index type, highly effective for both exact matches and range-based queries (e.g., greater than, less than, between).
Hash Index: Optimized specifically for high-speed, exact equality lookups.
GIN (Generalized Inverted Index): Designed for complex data types like full-text search and array columns.
GIST (Generalized Search Tree): Used for advanced, non-standard data types such as geospatial coordinates.
The Index Write Penalty

The Trade-off: While indexes dramatically speed up read queries, they impose a cost on write operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE).
Performance Impact: Every modification to the table must also be applied to all associated indexes. As a general rule, each additional index adds roughly 10%–30% overhead to write operations.
Strategy by Workload:
Read-Heavy (e.g., Social Media): Optimize for speed by creating comprehensive indexes.
Write-Heavy (e.g., Logging): Use minimal or no indexes to prevent performance bottlenecks.
Balanced (e.g., Chat Systems): Implement moderate, selective indexing on critical paths.
Best Practices

What to Index:
Columns used in WHERE clauses for filtering.
Columns used in JOIN conditions.
Columns used in ORDER BY clauses for sorting.
Frequently accessed primary or secondary lookup keys.
What NOT to Index:
Columns rarely used in queries.
Columns with very low cardinality (e.g., booleans with only true/false).
Tables that are exclusively write-heavy.
Verification: Use the EXPLAIN ANALYZE command to determine if your query is utilizing an index (Index Scan) or performing a full table read (Sequential Scan).
